    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d679">

                <pb facs="1377" n="1337" xml:id="pg1337"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">674.84A/9–2953: Telegram</note>
                <head>No. 679<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Egypt (Caffery) to the
                        Department of State</hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d679-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                        <lb/>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">NIACT</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Cairo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1953-09-29T14:00:00+02:00">September 29, 1953—2
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>379. As we have been reporting recently, Egyptians have been endeavoring to avoid
                    any sort of controversy with Israelis and have been playing down reports of
                    border violations, shooting of Bedouin, et cetera.</p>
                <p>This morning Vice President Abd Al Nasir asked to see me to tell me that last
                    night 300 Israeli troops occupied Al Auja in demilitarized zone and immediately
                    began digging trenches, putting up barbed wire, et cetera at a point one
                    kilometer northeast of the “building” at Al Auja. Egyptian liaison officer
                    protested in vain. Israelis retorted that Al Auja is a part of Israel.</p>
                <p>I counseled calmness and Nasir said they would be calm but in view of “gravity”
                    of what Israelis have done, and also in view of fact that Egyptians have
                    withdrawn most of their troops from Gaza district, he must immediately send
                    troops to area. I said “do not let them enter demilitarized zone”. He said “no
                    at least for the present they will stay on border”. “However”, he said, “I am
                    feaful of reaction of our public when they hear this and it will be impossible
                    to keep them from hearing it”.</p>
                <p>I told him that I would communicate on subject immediately with Department.</p>
                <p>I should appreciate it if Department would let me have as soon as possible some
                    word to pass back to Nasir.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
